                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#harmful_effect"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Tuberous_Sclerosis"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <reference>SNOMED:312850006</reference>
        <rdfs:label>Disease history</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Diseases_of_the_respiratory_system -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Diseases_of_the_respiratory_system">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Disease"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#harmful_effect"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/Parkinson_disease"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <Synonyms>disease or disorder of respiratory system</Synonyms>
        <Synonyms>disorder of respiratory system</Synonyms>
        <Synonyms>respiratory system disease</Synonyms>
        <Synonyms>respiratory system disease or disorder</Synonyms>
        <definition>A body system disease that occurs in different organs functioning in respiration and consisting especially of the nose, nasal passages, nasopharynx, larynx, trachea, bronchi, and lungs.

A non-neoplastic or neoplastic disorder that affects the respiratory system. Representative examples include pneumonia, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, pulmonary failure, lung adenoma, lung carcinoma, and tracheal carcinoma.
http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0000684</definition>
        <reference>EFO:0000684</reference>
        <rdfs:label>Diseases of the respiratory system</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Dopamine_dysregulation_syndrome -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Dopamine_dysregulation_syndrome">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Long_term_complication_of_medication"/>
        <Ontology1296772722296:Reference>O’Sullivan, S. S., Evans, A. H., &amp; Lees, A. J. (2009). Dopamine Dysregulation Syndrome. CNS drugs, 23(2), 157-170.</Ontology1296772722296:Reference>
        <Ontology1296772722296:synonyms>DDS</Ontology1296772722296:synonyms>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Dopamine dysregulation syndrome (DDS) is a relatively recently described iatrogenic disturbance that may complicate long-term symptomatic therapy of Parkinson&apos;s disease. Patients with DDS develop an addictive pattern of dopamine replacement therapy (DRT) use, administering doses in excess of those required to control their motor symptoms. </rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:label>Dopamine dysregulation syndrome</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Drinks -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Drinks">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Diet"/>
        <Synonyms>Drink</Synonyms>
        <definition>A single serving of a beverage; any liquid suitable for drinking.
http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCIT_C86036</definition>
        <reference>NCIT:C86036</reference>
        <rdfs:label>Drinks</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Dysarthria -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Dysarthria">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Motor_symptom_of_Parkinson_disease"/>
        <Ontology1296772722296:Reference>Mosley A.D., Romaine D.S. &amp; Samii A. (2010) The Encyclopedia of Parkinson&apos;s Disease. Infobase Publishing.</Ontology1296772722296:Reference>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Difficulties with speech that are the result of BRADYKINESIA involving the muscles of the mouth, throat, and chest.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:label>Dysarthria</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Dysarthria_and_dysphagia -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Dysarthria_and_dysphagia">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Bulbar_dysfunction"/>
        <definition>Dysarthria and dysphagia are the most common bulbar motor neuron symptoms and may be due either to UMN or LMN dysfunction. Dysarthria is frequently the initial bulbar symptom in ALS, but some patients may present with both dysarthria and dysphagia.

Dysarthria may result from LMN weakness of the lips, tongue, or palate. The speech is usually slurred and may have a nasal quality. Hoarseness may be present due to associated vocal cord weakness. UMN or spastic dysarthria produces a characteristically strained vocal quality with slow speech. The presence of both UMN and LMN features of dysarthria is a common bulbar finding suggestive of ALS.

Dysphagia results from tongue weakness with disruption of the oral phase of swallowing, pharyngeal constrictor weakness with disruption of the pharyngeal phase of swallowing, or both. Tongue weakness may lead to pocketing of food between the cheeks and gums. Tongue fasciculations may be seen in more than 50 percent. Pharyngeal weakness often manifests as coughing and choking on food, liquids, or secretions such as saliva or mucus. Aspiration may occur. UMN dysphagia results from slow and discoordinated contraction of the swallowing muscles, which may lead to coughing and choking.

https://www.uptodate.com/contents/clinical-features-of-amyotrophic-lateral-sclerosis-and-other-forms-of-motor-neuron-disease#H3992271448</definition>
        <rdfs:label>Dysarthria and dysphagia</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Dysexecutive_syndrome -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Dysexecutive_syndrome">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Neuropsychiatric_dysfunction"/>
        <Ontology1296772722296:Reference>Ceravolo, R., Pagni, C., Tognoni, G., &amp; Bonuccelli, U. (2012). The epidemiology and clinical manifestations of dysexecutive syndrome in Parkinson’s disease. Frontiers in neurology, 3.</Ontology1296772722296:Reference>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>A poorly defined nosological entity, which may be caused by various diseases. According to diagnostic criteria proposed by Godefroy et al. (2010), dysexecutive syndrome includes cognitive (e.g., deficits in response inhibition, set-shifting, and information generation) and/or behavioral (e.g., apathy, distractibility, perseverative behavior) alterations. </rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:label>Dysexecutive syndrome</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Dyskinesia -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Dyskinesia">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Long_term_complication_of_medication"/>
        <Ontology1296772722296:Reference>Hankey, G. J., &amp; Wardlaw, J. M. (2008). Clinical neurology. Manson publishing.</Ontology1296772722296:Reference>
        <Ontology1296772722296:synonyms>dyskinesias</Ontology1296772722296:synonyms>
        <Ontology1296772722296:synonyms>dyskinetic movements</Ontology1296772722296:synonyms>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Abnormal dystonic and choreoathetoid involuntary movements that may occur spontaneously after a sudden voluntary movement, after prolonged physical exertion, or during sleep.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Dyskinesia</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


