    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Facial_muscle_weakness">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Bulbar_dysfunction"/>
        <definition>Weakness of the muscles of the upper face may produce incomplete eye closure. Lower facial weakness is more common and may result in a poor lip seal that may contribute to dysarthria or to drooling and sialorrhea, particularly in patients with associated swallowing difficulty. Masseter muscle weakness can cause difficulty chewing; when severe, it may produce an inability to close the mouth. By contrast, weakness of the pterygoids may produce difficulty opening the mouth and moving the jaw from side to side. Severe weakness of both muscle groups may lead to disarticulation of the temporomandibular joint.

https://www.uptodate.com/contents/clinical-features-of-amyotrophic-lateral-sclerosis-and-other-forms-of-motor-neuron-disease#H3992271448</definition>
        <rdfs:label>Facial muscle weakness</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Falls -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Falls">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Motor_symptom_of_Parkinson_disease"/>
        <Ontology1296772722296:Reference>Mosley, A. D., Romaine, D. S., &amp; Samii, A. (2010). The Encyclopedia of Parkinson&apos;s Disease. Infobase Publishing.</Ontology1296772722296:Reference>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Falls are most likely to occur during changes in position— from sitting to standing, from standing to walking, when changing direction during movement, and from walking to sitting.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:label>Falls</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Fat_intake -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Fat_intake">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Dietary_pattern"/>
        <owl:disjointWith rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Mediterranean_diet(MeDi)"/>
        <Synonyms>Dietary fat intake</Synonyms>
        <Synonyms>Triglyceride</Synonyms>
        <Synonyms>fat intake measurement</Synonyms>
        <definition>Fats composed of three fatty acid chains linked to a glycerol molecule.
http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCIT_C906</definition>
        <definition>Quantification of the fat intake of an individual.
http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0010809</definition>
        <reference>EFO:0010809</reference>
        <reference>NCIT:C906</reference>
        <rdfs:label>Fat intake</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Fatty_acids -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Fatty_acids">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Nutrient_substance"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#harmful_effect"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/DOID_332"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <Synonyms>Fatty Acid</Synonyms>
        <Synonyms>Fatty Acids</Synonyms>
        <Synonyms>Fatty acid</Synonyms>
        <Synonyms>fatty acid</Synonyms>
        <definition>Monobasic, aliphatic acids with varying chain lengths between 6 and 30. Fatty acids are a major constituent in all fats and can be either saturated, monounsaturated, or polyunsaturated. Saturated and monounsaturated fatty acids are synthesized by the body, whereas polyunsaturated fatty acids are not and must be obtained in the diet. (NCI)
A major component of fats that are used by the body for energy and tissue development.
http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCIT_C492</definition>
        <reference>NCIT:C492</reference>
        <rdfs:label>Fatty acids</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Festination -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Festination">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Gait_disturbance"/>
        <Ontology1296772722296:Reference>Mosley, A. D., Romaine, D. S., &amp; Samii, A. (2010). The Encyclopedia of Parkinson&apos;s Disease. Infobase Publishing.</Ontology1296772722296:Reference>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>The Latin origin of the word festination means “to hasten,” as a person with this gait disturbance appears to do. A festinating gait consists of short, increasingly rapid steps with a marked forward lean, as though the person is about to break into a run.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:label>Festination</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Fiber -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Fiber">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Nutrient_substance"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#beneficial_effect"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/DOID_332"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <Synonyms>Dietary Fiber</Synonyms>
        <Synonyms>Fibers</Synonyms>
        <Synonyms>Fibra</Synonyms>
        <Synonyms>fiber</Synonyms>
        <Synonyms>fibers</Synonyms>
        <Synonyms>fibre</Synonyms>
        <definition>Compounds in food which are not digested by the digestive processes in the human stomach or small intestine.; In food, fiber is the part of fruits, vegetables, legumes, and whole grains that cannot be digested. The fiber in food may help prevent cancer.
http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCIT_C68492</definition>
        <reference>NCIT:C68492</reference>
        <rdfs:label>Fiber</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Fig -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Fig">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Fruit"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#beneficial_effect"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Alzheimer_disease"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <PMID>PMID:25050360</PMID>
        <Synonyms>Ficus carica</Synonyms>
        <Synonyms>common fig</Synonyms>
        <Synonyms>figs</Synonyms>
        <definition>4% figs treated AD Tg mice significantly attenuated oxidative damage, as evident by decreased lipid peroxidation and protein carbonyls and restoration of antioxidant status.</definition>
        <definition>Fruits with edible peel from the plant classified under the species Ficus carica L., commonly known as Figs.
http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/FOODON_03541647</definition>
        <reference>FOODON:03541647</reference>
        <rdfs:label>Fig</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Fish -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Fish">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Food"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#beneficial_effect"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/DOID_332"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#beneficial_effect"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Alzheimer_disease"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <PMID>PMID:12873849</PMID>
        <PMID>PMID:32019087</PMID>
        <Synonyms>edible fish</Synonyms>
        <Synonyms>fish consumption</Synonyms>
        <Synonyms>fish intake</Synonyms>
        <definition>A grouping of jawed and jawless vertebrate animals usually having fins and a covering of scales or plates, breathing by means of gills, and living almost entirely in the water.
CDISC Definition: Any jawed or jawless organisms in the phylum Chordata including the jawless fish, armored fish, cartilaginous fish, ray-finned fish and lobe-finned fish&quot;
 https://ncit.nci.nih.gov/ncitbrowser/ConceptReport.jsp?dictionary=NCI_Thesaurus&amp;ns=ncit&amp;code=C14207</definition>
        <description>Overall fish consumption: OR(95% CI): 0.27(0.12-0.60)
Conversely, there was a suggestion of an inverse association with overall fish consumption.
[url:https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32019087]

Once per week fish consumption: RR(95%CI): 0.5(0.2, 1)
Dietary intake of n-3 fatty acids and weekly consumption of fish may reduce the risk of incident Alzheimer disease.
[url:https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12873849]</description>
        <reference>NCIT:C14207</reference>
        <rdfs:label>Fish</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Fluctuating_dystonia -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Fluctuating_dystonia">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Dystonia"/>
        <Ontology1296772722296:Reference>Jankovic, J. J. T. E., &amp; Tolosa. (2007). Parkinson&apos;s disease and movement disorders. E. Tolosa (Ed.). Lippincott Williams &amp; Wilkins.</Ontology1296772722296:Reference>
        <Ontology1296772722296:synonyms>diurnal fluctuation</Ontology1296772722296:synonyms>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Variation in the severity of dystonia in the course of the day.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:label>Fluctuating dystonia</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Fluctuation -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Fluctuation">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Long_term_complication_of_medication"/>
        <Ontology1296772722296:Reference>Jankovic, J. (2005). Motor fluctuations and dyskinesias in Parkinson&apos;s disease: clinical manifestations. Movement Disorders, 20(S11), S11-S16.</Ontology1296772722296:Reference>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>luctuations in the symptoms of Parkinson&apos;s disease (PD), such as wearing-off and on-off effects, and dyskinesias are related to a variety of factors, including duration and dosage of levodopa, age at onset, stress, sleep, food intake, and other pharmacokinetic and pharmacodynamic mechanisms. </rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:label>Fluctuation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Focal_dystonia -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Focal_dystonia">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Primary_dystonia"/>
        <Ontology1296772722296:Reference>http://www.dystonia-foundation.org/</Ontology1296772722296:Reference>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Focal dystonias are adult-onset forms that affects a specific area of the body.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:label>Focal dystonia</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Food -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Food">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Diet"/>
        <Synonyms>Foods</Synonyms>
        <Synonyms>Foodstuff</Synonyms>
        <Synonyms>food groups</Synonyms>
        <Synonyms>food intake</Synonyms>
        <definition>A group of materials of either plant, animal or artificial origin containing essential body nutrients that can be ingested by an organism to produce energy, stimulate growth, and maintain life.
http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCIT_C62695</definition>
        <reference>NCIT:C62695</reference>
        <rdfs:label>Food</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Food_additives -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Food_additives">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Food"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#beneficial_effect"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Alzheimer_disease"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <Synonyms>Nutrition, Food Additives</Synonyms>
        <Synonyms>food additive</Synonyms>
        <definition>Substances used in the processing or storage of foods or animal feed including ANTIOXIDANTS; FOOD PRESERVATIVES; FOOD COLORING AGENTS; FLAVORING AGENTS; ANTI-INFECTIVE AGENTS; EXCIPIENTS and other similarly used substances. Many of the same substances are used as PHARMACEUTIC AIDS.
http://id.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/D005503</definition>
        <reference>mesh:D005503</reference>
        <rdfs:label>Food additives</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Foot_drag -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Foot_drag">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Gait_disturbance"/>
        <Ontology1296772722296:Reference>Mosley, A. D., Romaine, D. S., &amp; Samii, A. (2010). The Encyclopedia of Parkinson&apos;s Disease. Infobase Publishing.</Ontology1296772722296:Reference>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>People with Parkinson’s often drag one foot or sometimes both feet, depending on whether movement dysfunctions are unilateral (primarily affect one side) or bilateral (affect both sides). Foot drag involves both MOTOR and PROPRIOCEPTION dysfunctions.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:label>Foot drag</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Foot_dystonia -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Foot_dystonia">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Focal_dystonia"/>
        <Ontology1296772722296:Reference>Jankovic, J. J. T. E., &amp; Tolosa. (2007). Parkinson&apos;s disease and movement disorders. E. Tolosa (Ed.). Lippincott Williams &amp; Wilkins.</Ontology1296772722296:Reference>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Foot dystonia is uncontrollable and often painful muscle contractions in the foot which causes a twisting or turning in of the foot.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:label>Foot dystonia</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Football -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Football">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Physical_activity"/>
