        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#beneficial_effect"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/Parkinson_disease"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#harmful_effect"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/DOID_9255"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#harmful_effect"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/Parkinson_disease"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#harmful_effect"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Alzheimer_disease"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <Synonyms>Receptor Protein</Synonyms>
        <Synonyms>receptor</Synonyms>
        <definition>A protein located on the cell surface, or in the cytoplasm, that binds to a specific signaling factor, such as a hormone, antigen, or neurotransmitter, causing a conformational and functional change in the receptor molecule. The ligand-bound receptor then alters its interaction with target molecules, which leads to changes in cellular physiology through modification of the activity of one or more signal transduction pathways.
http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCIT_C18106</definition>
        <reference>NCIT:C18106</reference>
        <rdfs:label>Receptor</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Red_meat -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Red_meat">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Meat"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#beneficial_effect"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Alzheimer_disease"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#harmful_effect"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/Parkinson_disease"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <PMID>PMID:34494887</PMID>
        <Synonyms>consumption of red meat</Synonyms>
        <Synonyms>dark meat</Synonyms>
        <definition>PMID:33748832</definition>
        <definition>The group includes carcass meat of domestic animals (e.g. beef, veal, pork, mutton / lamb, horse, rabbit) and game (e.g. wild pig, boar, venison, whale).
http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/FOODON_03400794</definition>
        <description>50-g/d increment in unprocessed red meat(unprocessed beef, lamb, and pork) intake: HR(95% CI): 0.70(0.53-0.92)
Red meat intake was associated with reduced risks of all-cause dementia and AD.
[url:https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33748832/]</description>
        <description>Consuming red meat always: OR(95% CI): 4.47(1.67-11.94)
Consuming red meat sometimes: OR(95% CI): 2.15(1.06-4.39)
Consuming red meat sometimes or always was positively associated with Parkinson&apos;s disease as compared to eating red meats rarely or never.
[url:https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34494887/]</description>
        <reference>FOODON:03400794</reference>
        <rdfs:label>Red meat</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


