            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <PMID>PMID:4977122</PMID>
        <Synonyms>consumption of sausage</Synonyms>
        <Synonyms>sausage meat</Synonyms>
        <Synonyms>sausages</Synonyms>
        <definition>A food product that is highly seasoned minced meat and is encased in a skin in the shape of a cylinder.
http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/FOODON_03315904</definition>
        <description>&lt; 2 times/months: OR(95%CI): 3.9 (1.1, 13.9)
&gt;= 2 times/months: OR(95%CI): 5.4 (1.4, 20.0)
Using control self-responses, consumption of hot dogs, sausage, pepperoni, kielbasa, &quot;other&quot; canned meat, poultry liver, any stomach/intestine, beef stomach/intestine, any organ tissue, and beef organ tissue was individually associated with increased sCJD risk.</description>
        <reference>FOODON:03315904</reference>
        <rdfs:label>Sausage</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Secondary_dystonia -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Secondary_dystonia">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Continual_dystonia"/>
        <Ontology1296772722296:Reference>Jankovic, J. J. T. E., &amp; Tolosa. (2007). Parkinson&apos;s disease and movement disorders. E. Tolosa (Ed.). Lippincott Williams &amp; Wilkins.</Ontology1296772722296:Reference>
        <Ontology1296772722296:synonyms>acquired dystonia</Ontology1296772722296:synonyms>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Secondary dystonia results from environmental or other damage to the brain, or from exposure to certain types of medications.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:label>Secondary dystonia</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Segmental_dystonia -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Segmental_dystonia">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Primary_dystonia"/>
        <Ontology1296772722296:Reference>Jankovic, J. J. T. E., &amp; Tolosa. (2007). Parkinson&apos;s disease and movement disorders. E. Tolosa (Ed.). Lippincott Williams &amp; Wilkins.</Ontology1296772722296:Reference>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Segmental dystonia affects two or more adjacent parts of the body.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:label>Segmental dystonia</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Sensory_symptom -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Sensory_symptom">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Non-motor_symptom_of_Amyotrophic_lateral_sclerosis"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Non-motor_symptom_of_Parkinson_disease"/>
        <Ontology1296772722296:Reference>Jankovic, J. J. T. E., &amp; Tolosa. (2007). Parkinson&apos;s disease and movement disorders. E. Tolosa (Ed.). Lippincott Williams &amp; Wilkins.</Ontology1296772722296:Reference>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Sensory symptoms are part of PD in the earliest clinical descriptions of the disorder and are reported in 40%-50% of patients.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:label>Sensory symptom</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Sexual_dysfunction -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Sexual_dysfunction">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Non-motor_symptom_of_Parkinson_disease"/>
        <Ontology1296772722296:Reference>Tuite, P. J. (2009). Parkinson&apos;s Disease: A Guide to Patient Care. Springer Publishing Company.</Ontology1296772722296:Reference>
        <Ontology1296772722296:synonyms>genitourinary dysfunction</Ontology1296772722296:synonyms>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Sexual dysfunction may involve a reduction in sex drive with difficulty becoming aroused, difficulty achieving and sustaining an erection, inability to reach orgasm, or pain with sexual activity. </rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:label>Sexual dysfunction</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Shuffling_gait -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Shuffling_gait">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/NDDRFO#Gait_disturbance"/>
        <Ontology1296772722296:Reference>Mosley, A. D., Romaine, D. S., &amp; Samii, A. (2010). The Encyclopedia of Parkinson&apos;s Disease. Infobase Publishing.</Ontology1296772722296:Reference>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Walking stooped forward with short, shuffling steps is perhaps the most distinctive gait disturbance of Parkinson’s. In this gait, the person’s feet barely clear the surface when walking, or one or both feet may drag.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:label>Shuffling gait</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


